(PRC-77: `to process <pid>` reaches exactly one process, numbered 1 to
 2147483647; a pid known only when the program runs that is outside the
 range sets the error flag and sends nothing (LANGUAGE.md:4584,
 4593-4597). Each pid below is computed, so the compiler cannot prove it
 and the check happens at run time. The kernel's group meaning has its
 own form, `to my process group` (LANGUAGE.md:4580, 4588-4589).
 Up to vox 0.4.15 both pids reached kill(2) unfiltered as broadcasts and
 this probe printed "pid 0 reached kill(2) and returned success" and the
 same for pid -1 (Discrepancy 5, resolved by vox #279). A literal 0 or -1
 is now a compile error: D5.vox.
 SAFETY: this probe sends signal 0 and ONLY signal 0, which the kernel
 never delivers, to the pids and to the program's own process group.
 Re-recorded 2026-10-13 on vox main a0a2ecc.
 Ran, from the repo root:
   VOX_CORE_PATH=/home/josj/scr/english/vox/coreasm \
   /home/josj/scr/english/vox/target/release/vox \
   docs/ledger/probes/process-control/PRC-77.vox -o p && ./p
 expected output:
   pid 0 set the error flag and sent nothing
   pid -1 set the error flag and sent nothing
   my process group accepted signal 0
)
a number called 'the group pid' is 5.
Set 'the group pid' to 'the group pid' subtract 5.
Send signal 0 to process 'the group pid'.
On error print "pid 0 set the error flag and sent nothing".
a number called 'the broadcast pid' is 5.
Set 'the broadcast pid' to 'the broadcast pid' subtract 6.
Send signal 0 to process 'the broadcast pid'.
On error print "pid -1 set the error flag and sent nothing".
Send signal 0 to my process group.
On error print "unexpected: my own process group refused signal 0".
print "my process group accepted signal 0".
